  "query_preview": "..\n\nMid level short wave ridging overhead will depart eastward across \nthe Lower Great Lakes tonight. Residual mid level subsidence will \nsupport mostly clear skies through the evening. With the eastward \ndeparture of sfc high pressure, an increase in the SSW gradient \ntonight will limit radiational cooling, warranting milder nighttime \ntemps compared to the last few nights; forecast lows in mid 50s to \nlow 60s.\n\nAn axis of deep layer moisture now extending across the central \nplains into northern Ontario will experience renewed tropical \nmoisture transport due to the remnants of Hurricane Polo and a \ncontinued influx off the western gulf. A progressive northern stream \ntrough axis and mid level height falls across northern Ontario into \nthe northern Great Lakes tonight will drive this moist axis along \nwith a mid level tropospheric front across southern Michigan on \nWednesday. Mid level short wave impulses within the mid level theta \ne advection will support moderate to high chances for rain through \nthe day Wed. Lingering low level dry air will keep the rain light \nand relatively high based. Strengthening upper jet dynamics and an \nincrease in the low level inflow will tighten the frontal feature \noverhead Wed night into Thursday and will likely drive some \nsemblance of a surface warm front toward Se Mi. This will support \ncontinued rain chances and may allow temps near the Ohio border to \npush 80 degrees Thursday.\n\nAlthough an upper wave over the southern plains is forecast to \nrapidly weaken as it lifts northeast Thursday into Fri, model PV \nsurfaces indicate phasing between northern and southern stream jet \nstreaks to the north and northeast of the Great Lakes. This will \nresult in intensification of mid level deformation across srn Lower \nMi Thurs afternoon into early Fri. Model solutions indicate a \ncorridor of very high moisture feeding into the elevated frontal \nsystem. Precipitable water values across Se Mi are forecast to range \nfrom 1.6 to 2.0 inches Thurs/Fri, within the 99th percentile for \nearly October PWATs if not pushing record territory. While weak \nelevated instability may support some convective elements within the \nrain, the kinematics will be supportive of a band of moderate to \npossibly heavy rainfall. At this stage in the forecast there is \nstill uncertainty as to the precise location of the more persistent \nforcing within the DTX forecast area. Probabilistic guidance \nindicates moderate to high chances for total rainfall between 0.5 \nand 1.5 inches Thurs into Fri, with low probabilities for rainfall \ntotals of 2 or more inches. A northern stream trough is forecast to \ndrive the deep layer moist axis south of the area during the day \nFriday, with much cooler and drier air advection following the \npassage of the associated surface front. Broad high pressure will \nexpand across the area Saturday, leading to a cool and dry start to \nthe weekend.",
